## Why is it important to distinguish between general Criminal Justice Information (CJI) and Criminal History Record Information (CHRI) when managing user access and loggin?

# Regulatory Mandates & Privacy Protections
**The CFR Baseline–** Under the FBI CJIS Security Policy, Criminal Justice Information (CJI) acts as a generally broad umbrella term for all data necessary for law enforcement & civil agencies to carry out their missions.
Simply put, **Criminal Record Information** (CHRI)(which would cover arrests. charges, and dispositions) is a sensitive subset of CJI. Due to the heightened sensitivity, CHRI is ultimately governed by strict federal regulations under **Title 28, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Part 20** and the **NCIC Operating Manual.** Accessing, using, or disseminating CHRI improperly is a serious offense that can face state and federal criminal penalties or severe administrative sanctions–a.k.a immediate termination of services. 
**NARA Marking Standards–** Essentially, how labeling and handling works. Under the NARA Controlled Unclassified Information (CUI) framework, standard CJI categories (like biometric//biographic data) are marked with a standard CUI banner, where CHRI//Identity history data would require specialized, highly restricted marking of **CUI//SP-CHRI**
# Divergent Access Control + Use Cases (Restricted use vs non-restricted)
**NCIC Restricted Files–** The interstate Identitication Index(III) and certain NCIC Restricted Files(Say... Gang Files, Threat Screening Center Files, National Sex Offender Registry, and Violent Person Files) are protected as... CHRI. Under CJISPol, agencies are absolutely prohibited from disseminating files restricted or CHRI for purposes other than law enforcement. 
# Crucial Logging and Auditing Differences
**Secondary Dissemination Logging–** tis one of the most critical operational reasons to separate the twain. Under control **CA-3 (Information Exchange)**, an agency must log the dissemination of CHRI when released unto an authorized agency that is not part of the primary information exchange agreement. Passive compliance makes the system sleep; however, the log ensures the encode-decode loop remains unbroken.
**Enhanced Audit Log Content for III–** Under **AU-3 (Content of Audit Records), audit logs for queries to the Interstate Identificaiton Index(III)–which serves as the primary system for exchanging CHRI–must meet heightened, explicit reporting criteria. The III portion of the log must clearly identify the operator, the autorized receiving agency, the requestor, and the secondary recipient. This detailed, four-part metadata capture is not required for general CJI log files, meaning logging mechanisms must differentiate transactions based on the data type being queried.
# Managing Compliance Scope & Vendor Risks
- **Audit Timelines for Contractors–** Third-party private contractors who get CHRI under an outsourcing agreement should undergo their first CJIS audit within **one year of first receipt** and remain prepared for unannounced inspections. This accelerated audit cadence does not apply in the same manner to vendors with incidental or non-CHRI access.
- **What Lies Outside the Scope?–** Standalone transactional number(e.g., ORI, NIC, or UCN) are excempt from CJI protections if they are not accompanied by identifying information. More or less, standalone PII extracted from CJI is managed under local privacy controls and is ultimately not subject to the strict auditing and logging requirements of the CJIS security policy. Distinguishing these elements of data allows agencies to overall harden their system authorization boundaries and avoid unecessarily expanding expensive "CJIS compliance scope". 
